== review-report.md format ==
# Review Report — Round {{.Round}}
## Summary
PASS / FAIL
## Checklist
| Item | Status | Notes |
## Issues
### [SEVERITY] Rule — file/path
Description
## Verdict
PASS / FAIL / CONDITIONAL PASS

== Severity ==
- critical/HIGH: blocks transition, must fix
- warning/MEDIUM: should fix
- low/INFO: log only

== Common Pitfalls ==
- If the diff adds a new constant, enum value, or category: check whether the SAME file (or closely related files) has hardcoded lists, CLI flag usage strings, error messages, or map keys that enumerate the same set. Flag any that were not updated in sync.
- If the diff modifies a config/setting struct or merge logic: verify the merged value is actually consumed at the call site (not just tested in isolation with a mock).
- If this round's (or a prior round's) escalation.json shows `needed` flipped from true to false with a note claiming the blocker was resolved, and no later round elaborates on it: do NOT take the note at face value. Re-run the verification the note describes and cite the fresh output as evidence, or mark the checklist item [UNVERIFIABLE] with the reason — never mark it PASS on the note's word alone.
- If the diff adds or changes test assertions: check whether the expected value is copied from the same formula/constant the implementation uses (a tautological assertion that cannot catch a regression), or whether an idempotency/retry test manually resets state to fake a fresh initial condition instead of driving two consecutive real inputs. Flag either as a test-coverage issue.

== Evidence Requirement ==
Do NOT take coder-report.md claims on faith. Every checklist item you mark PASS must cite at
least one piece of first-hand evidence from the diff in review-package.md (quote the hunk or
file:line in the Notes column). A claim you could not verify from the diff belongs under
[UNVERIFIABLE], not PASS.

== UNVERIFIABLE_FROM_DIFF ==

Some requirements cannot be verified from the current diff alone, e.g.:
- Cross-task integration behavior
- Runtime / performance characteristics
- Items that require actually running tests to confirm
- Behavior involving files not touched in this round

For these:
1. Mark them as `[UNVERIFIABLE]`, explaining why they cannot be verified
2. Do NOT run extensive git/grep/cat commands exploring the codebase just to verify these
3. These items do NOT count toward the PASS/FAIL verdict (not a warning or critical)
4. They will be carried forward to the acceptor layer

== Incremental Writing & Resume ==
Write review-report.md incrementally — create the file with the header and empty table first,
then update it after each checklist item reviewed. Do not accumulate everything in memory and
write once at the end.

Before starting, check if review-report.md already exists. A previous session may have been
interrupted mid-review. If it exists, read it:
- Items already in the checklist table with a Status → already reviewed, skip them
- Empty rows or missing items → review these (this is where the previous session stopped)
- A file with only headers or an empty table means resume from the first item
- If Verdict is already filled → the review is complete, verify it's consistent and finish
Do NOT assume an existing file means the review is done — check which items have results.

